# Graph

A graph in HQL is an ordinary value with two type parameters — what its nodes
are, and what its edges are:

```hql
type Graph[N, E]
```

It is not a rendering, not a keyword and not a fixed structure the language
knows about. `Graph` and `Edge` belong to the **graph extension**; HMD and
knowledge each specialize them without either owning the other. The layering is
core → graph → knowledge, with document sources beside it.

## The edge is the primitive

```hql
type Edge[S, T] {
    source: S
    target: T
    data:   Data
}
```

Three things follow from that shape, and they are why the graph model stays
small.

**Source and target are typed separately.** `Edge[S, T]` rather than `Edge[N]`
admits an edge whose ends are different kinds of thing — a card pointing at a
concept, a concept supported by a document — without a union type at the node
position.

**The payload is [[data-type|Data]].** An edge carries a tree, and the keys in it
are the author's. `relation: parent` is an entry, not grammar, which is what
keeps every vocabulary a vault invents out of the language — see [[link-op]] for
the `[[..]] -> [[..]] {..}` form that writes one.

**Carrying data is not being typed.** An edge annotated `relation: parent` is not
thereby a `Parent` edge. `typed Relation` has to check a contract; a key in a
tree is an author's claim. This is the same rule that keeps a `type:` entry in a
header from minting a type — see [[card-type]].

## Two specializations, one mechanism

```hql
type Link[S, T]     <: Edge[S, T]
type HmdGraph       <: Graph[Card, Link]

type Relation[S, T] <: Edge[S, T]
type KnowledgeGraph <: Graph[Concept, Relation]
```

| Graph | Nodes | Edges | Comes from |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Document graph | documents | authored links | the vault's own structure |
| Knowledge graph | concepts | relations | interpretation, and it must be performed |

`[[alice]] -> [[project-x]]` is an edge in the first. `WorksOn(alice, project_x)`
is an edge in the second. They render through the same machinery without being
the same object, and that is the point of parameterizing `Graph` rather than
declaring one graph type with a `kind` field. A document graph is discovered; a
knowledge graph is argued for.

## A relation is an edge, and a card may represent it

`Relation` is a first-class knowledge type with one supertype:

```hql
type Relation[S, T] <: Edge[S, T]
```

An edge has a source, a target and data. A relation has all three and adds
identity, evidence and provenance, so the narrowing is ordinary. What it does
**not** narrow is `Card`. A relation can be imported from another namespace,
derived by a rule or read out of a database, and in each of those cases there is
a relation and no document anywhere, so a relation cannot be a kind of card.

A **Relation Card** is a card that represents one, giving it a body, a name in
the vault and incoming links of its own:

```hql
card.metadata.relation : Relation[S, T]
```

That is refinement of the card's knowledge layer, not a second parent — see
[[type-system]], which separates the two mechanisms.

It also marks where `<:` stops. `Edge[S, T]` is what a relation *is*. Being
renderable is not: that is a capability, supplied by an extension as
`Renderable[Html]` rather than by inheritance.

> This replaces an earlier reading on this card and on [[card-type]] that a
> Relation Card had two supertypes, `{Card, Edge[S, T]}`. The supertype-set
> syntax stands on its own; this case does not motivate it.

## Endpoint identity, and where it fails

[[link-op]] settles identity for the document graph: an edge is identified by its
endpoints, so authoring the same link twice updates one annotation rather than
adding a second edge. That makes the document graph a simple graph, which is
right for it — two `[[bob]]` links in `alice` are one dependency stated twice.

A knowledge graph cannot be keyed that way. Distinct Relation Cards may make
conflicting claims about the same pair, and knowledge must retain both with their
provenance and evidence rather than merging them into one annotated edge — see
[[hql/knowledge]]. The knowledge graph is therefore a multigraph whose edges have
identity of their own, and that identity belongs to the relation itself rather
than to any document that happens to represent it.

So the two graphs differ in a way worth stating plainly: **the document graph is
keyed by endpoints, the knowledge graph by edge identity.** [[hql/collections]]
already warns that set membership must not equate distinct claims because they
share endpoints; this is that warning with a type behind it.

## Direction and traversal

An edge is directed — it has a source and a target — and `uplinks` and
`downlinks` are the two directions of traversal over one edge set, not two edge
sets. A node can be its own source and target. See [[doc-type]].

Whether `Graph` exposes its nodes and edges as collections, and which ones, is
open:

```hql
graph.nodes : Set[N]
graph.edges : Set[E]
```

`Set` follows [[hql/collections]], since discovering edges must not establish an
ordering guarantee. This card writes generics in square brackets, following the
design conversation and [[type-system]], while
[[hql/collections]] writes `Set<T>`; that divergence is unsettled everywhere and
this card does not settle it.

What is less clear is whether a graph over a vault should be materialized at
all, or whether it is a query that a source may execute — a graph store, a
database, or the vault index — which is the same execution
freedom [[pipes]] reserves for collection stages.

## Projection and presentation

```hql
knowledge
| graph
```

`graph` here is an ordinary function from a knowledge value to a `Graph`, not a
presentation keyword. The resulting value happens to have a renderer registered
by the graph extension, which is what makes it appear in a host as a picture.

That reading resolves the tension [[hql/knowledge]] records between `graph` as a
projection and `graph` as a terminal presenter: the projection produces the
value, and rendering is a capability of the value. It does not resolve the
naming — whether the projection and the view should share a name is still a
decision, and `HmdGraph` currently reads as though it were a presentation type
when it is a graph over cards.

## Open questions

- Variance. `HmdGraph <: Graph[Card, Link]`, and `Card <: Doc`, so does
  `Graph[Card, Link] <: Graph[Doc, Edge]` hold? A function written over
  `Graph[Doc, Edge]` is useless if it does not. Covariance is defensible while
  graphs are immutable values, and unsound the moment a graph can be extended in
  place, so this is a question about whether graph construction is pure.
- Node identity. Two documents, two concepts, a document and the concept it
  describes: when are they the same node? `Set[N]` presumes an answer.
- Whether nodes must be values in the graph at all, or whether an edge's
  endpoints are references that the graph never resolves — the forward-link case
  from [[link-op]] argues for references.
- What a graph's horizon is. `cards` stops at the vault; a graph built from
  imported namespaces or an external store may not.
- Whether `Graph` is one type or a family with a shared surface, and whether
  traversal, subgraph selection and path queries are functions over it or a
  separate query vocabulary.

See [[link-op]] for the edge-writing operator, [[type-system]] for what `<:`
does and does not cover, [[card-type]] for the card that may represent a
relation, [[hql/knowledge]] for the semantic universe, [[hql/collections]] for the node and
edge collections and [[hql/design-status]] for what remains undecided.
